\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Referee Report, November 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Within economics, there is a growing area of research that studies the role
 significant life experiences have in determining life outcomes.
 While early childhood years have been an important focus, there has been
 very little study in the existing economic literature concerning how intense
 psychological experiences in young adulthood affect future outcomes.
 Military service is an example of a noteworthy event undertaken by a large
 fraction of young males across the globe.
 This paper specifically analyzes the effect military training has on the
 propensity of former military personnel to engage in violent crime.
 Previous studies, such as Rohlfs (2010) and Lindo & Stoeker (forthcoming),
 have attempted to measure the effect of military training on violence,
 but it has been argued that combat is the mechanism driving many of those
 results.
 While Galiani et al.
 (2011) is one previous paper that studied the effects of non-deployed military
 service on overall crime, the estimates for violent crime were not statisticall
y significant.
 Thus, the authors of this paper add to the existing literature because
 a formal link between army training and violence has not yet been established.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated before, the primary aim of the paper was to estimate the effect
 of army training (not deployment, or combat) on various measures of individual
 crime perpetration, especially violent crime (Siminski et al., this paper,
 p.
 17).
 Even though the analysis focused on years when conscription lotteries were
 implemented, the authors still faced a potential endogeneity problem because
 army service was not randomly assigned.
 Many conscripts were screened-out after a medical examination, while a
 number of service men joined the army voluntarily.
 Consequently, some unobservable determinants of crime perpetration may
 have been correlated with army training, so a standard OLS regression would
 have yielded biased estimates.
 Instead, the authors employed an instrumental variables approach to estimate
 the true effect by exploiting the randomness of conscription lotteries.
 Between 1965 and 1972, 15 biannual birth cohorts were subjected to conscription
 lotteries, conducted by date of birth, and no one from the last three cohorts
 was deployed (Siminski et al., this paper, p.
 6).
 So, the authors took advantage of the exogenous variation in the probability
 of military service resulting from the binary conscription lottery ballot
 outcome.
 Also, the effect of the ballot outcome was allowed to vary between cohorts,
 which lead to an over-identified 2SLS model.
 The primary identification assumptions of the paper were the following:
 the ballot outcome was correlated with army training (relevance condition);
 the only effect the ballot outcome had on crime perpetration was through
 its effect on army training (exclusion restriction).
 The outcome data were drawn from criminal cases with male defendants documented
 in New South Wales, Victoria, and Queensland from 1994 to 2010.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors constructed three distinct specifications in order to conduct
 their analysis.
 In the preferred analysis, the sample was restricted to the three youngest
 cohorts because they were not deployed to any combat area.
 The second specification repeats the previous procedure, but the sample
 is extended to the full set of 15 birth cohorts.
 The estimates from this specification are treated as upper bounds on the
 effect of army training because the authors assume deployment to Vietnam
 did not reduce subsequent crime perpetration, on average.
 Finally, the authors obtain estimates from a third specification, using
 all 15 cohorts, in which they separately identify the effect of army training
 and the effect of deployment to Vietnam.
 This strategy relied on the fact that the probability of deployment varied
 considerably between cohorts of conscripts.
 Tables 3, 4, and 5 report the results for each specification just describted,
 respectively.
 For all three specifications, the authors find little empirical evidence
 that military training increases the rate of civilian violence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Main Concerns and Comments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Administrative data was drawn from criminal court cases heard during 1994-2010
 in New South Wales, Victoria, and Queensland.
 The three identified states contain over 77% of the Australian population,
 but the remaining 23% of the population was not represented in the data.
 In fact, the authors did not even explain why the remaining Australian
 states were excluded.
 It seems as though the sample of cohorts being evaluated originate from
 all over the country, but these individuals could have committed crimes
 in any of the excluded states.
 Because the outcome data is restricted to a specific portion of the population,
 this could present some selection issues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In Section 2, the authors stated that permanent personality shifts due to
 military training shifts was questionable.
 However, they failed to consider the effect time has on changes in personality.
 It is plausible that the effects of military training are more prominent
 during the earlier years of adulthood, and then they decrease over time.
 Visual presentations of the reduced-form results show an increasing trend
 from left to right in the ratio of crimes to population, by birth cohort
 and ballot outcome.
 The authors claimed that this trend arose because the data availability
 window covers a different age range for each cohort.
 Though, why would differences in age cause this difference? As the results
 from Lindo & Stoecker (forthcoming) suggest, there is a diminishing marginal
 effect of military service on violent crime.
 So, this paper may be disregarding heterogenous time effects because they
 do not allow the effect of military training to change over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What is the explanation for not including state and/or region fixed effects?
 If the United States is any indication, then the various states and/or
 regions in Australia probably have different criminal laws, court procedures,
 crime rates, conviction rates, philosophies, political biases, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors show the first-stage regression results in Table 2 in order
 to demonstrate that their instrument is relevant and not subject to the
 problems associated with weak instruments.
 However, the authors do not perform any empirical tests to convince the
 reader that their instrument satisfies the exclusion restriction.
 While it is reasonable to believe that the binary conscription lottery
 ballot outcomes were randomly assigned, it still would be beneficial to
 see tests that verify this conjecture.
 Note that the random assignment of ballot outcomes implies that, in the
 population, the distribution of observable characteristics is identical
 for cohorts who were conscripted and those who were not.
 The identifying assumption and the entire analysis of the paper hinges
 on the fact that ballot outcomes were randomly assigned.
 As a result, the authors should include descriptive statistics of the included
 observable characteristics.
 Most importantly, a comparison of means test should be included as a way
 to test for random assignment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Minor Concerns and Comments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors presented an overly drawn out explanation of the potential connectio
ns between military training and violent crime.
 In addition, they provided a thorough description of National Service in
 Australia.
 For over 10 pages, the authors explained the possible reasons why exposure
 to military training, specifically, could lead a former soldier to perpetuate
 violent crime.
 Sections 2 and 3 could easily be reduced without hindering the overall
 message conveyed by the authors.
 Instead, I would suggest extending Sections 4 and 5, which outline methods
 and data, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Did the authors control for any individual-level determinants of crime perpetrat
ion on the right-hand side of the linear function? If not, what is their
 reasoning for omitting these control variables?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors did not present estimates of the treatment effect from a simple
 OLS regression.
 They described two scenarios that could cause the OLS estimation to yield
 biased and inconsistent estimates of the treatment effect.
 However, the actual OLS results would have confirmed their reasoning, and
 the OLS results could have been compared with the results from the three
 2SLS specifications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Visual presentations of the reduced-form results are discussed in Section
 6.2, but this graphical summary is insufficient.
 It is often common practice to include the actual results from the reduced-form
 estimation in order to compare them with the 2SLS estimates.
 In fact, there are distinct advantages to the reduced-form approach, such
 as obtaining unbiased OLS estimates, even in finite samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the second-stage, recent migrants were unable to be excluded from the
 crime database.
 To alleviate this problem, the first-stage data included synthesized records
 for migrants who arrived in Australia after the age of 20.
 How were these synthesized records constructed? Siminski and Ville (2012),
 which used the same approach, was cited by the authors, but a brief discussion
 on the synthesized records would be helpful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Individual-level data was unavailable in the second-stage regression.
 Instead, the authors utilized a group-means approach to obtain point estimates
 identical to that of individual data, as well as consistently estimated
 standard errors.
 Further explanation of the specific GLS procedure would be helpful, such
 as the estimation of the standard errors, and the exact calculation of
 the GLS weights.
 Moreover, the subscripts in equations (2) and (4) on the treatment variable
 do not match.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As a robustness test, the authors showed alternate 2SLS estimates in Tables
 6 and 7 that were generated using just-identified models, which were identical
 to the models used for Tables 3 and 4, respectively.
 The estimates were similar to the main results, but less precise.
 For other robustness tests, the authors can conduct a test of over-identifying
 moment restrictions.
\end_layout

